start with the travel chaos whose fault  is it well i think it's a combination of  everyone's fault but it's quite clear  the government had been asleep at the  wheel  i think yesterday the transport  secretary was spending most of his time  phoning around conservative mps trying  to get them to support the prime  minister then doing the job that he's  paid to do but it's quite clear that the  travel industry has been in trouble for  some time in terms of trying to recruit  staff and we were saying a way back last  year when restrictions were being lifted  and furlough was coming to an end that  those industries that were hardest hit  and longest to recover needed more  support and didn't get any and this is  the consequence of that  not directly into making sure that they  could scale up workforce that could  retain the staff that they require that  they were paying people  properly and look the airlines take a  huge amount of responsibility for this  there's no doubt that for example  british airways who were hiring and  re-firing were filing and rehiring  and all the issues they've had over the  last few years with pensions it's very  difficult to recruit staff when you're  doing that to your own staff at those  times so there's blame of pushing  everywhere here but in terms of the  government's role they need to bring  people together and to step in and do  what they can to help and my own airport  edinburgh are asking the government to  not relax the rules in terms of security  go to go either side but to make them  quicker so they can get staff onto the  front line as quickly as possible so  everyone needs to come together here now  to try and resolve this problem because  families holidays are being ruined and  we can see that from the pictures on the  sky over the last few days you mentioned  uh grant chapston what you might might  not have been doing yesterday we heard  from dominic raab whose deputy this  morning the prime minister's deputy  saying there's nothing to see here it's  everything's fine  well everything's not fine because the  government's paralyzed by this issue and  have been for the last  five or six months now and you see the  report also today from lord guyt who's  the advisor on ethics to the prime  minister who got his job because the  previous incumbent resigned over the  prime minister whitewashing a report  into bullying in the home office and  he's saying that the prime minister is  ridiculing the ministerial code that he  has to come out and explain why he  doesn't think he has to resign because  he's broken the ministerial code all of  the things are just dominating our  politics at the moment when you have  travel chaos and when you have a cost of  living crisis it's the worst in a  generation yeah but the government's  come up with 15 billion pounds to try to  help with with that is what they would  say in response well they would but it's  too little too late for many and every  every penny is obviously welcome but  they've they've actually had to come up  with the ideas that they've been  ridiculing labor for since january when  we came up with the windfall tax tax and  oil and gas companies to help people  with their energy bills and to do  everything they possibly can for the  cost of living crisis but you can see  what's happening at the moment  everyone's spending their time in  government trying to defend the prime  minister we may or may not have a  leadership challenge  next week we've got a prime minister  who's really a lame duck and the only  way to resolve any of this issue is for  the prime minister to resign and to  replace him with someone who can bring  integrity back i mean he's come up with  15 billion quid which is more than  labour uh had suggested when you were  asking for the windfall tax and he is  also being lauded by the president of  ukraine  uh well there's  the country's been at war before  involved in wars before and changed the  prime minister but we can see 